1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,173
When revenue drops, a small online seller gets a chart, not an answer.

2
00:00:05,173 --> 00:00:10,420
WhyDrop is an agent that explains why, and proves every claim it makes.

3
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:17,521
The clues are scattered across order exports, ad spend, web traffic, and a refunds database.

4
00:00:17,521 --> 00:00:24,966
Most sellers have no analyst to join them up, and a chatbot that guesses can send them after the wrong fix.

5
00:00:25,666 --> 00:00:29,378
Zetaris federates all of it into one SQL layer.

6
00:00:29,378 --> 00:00:31,905
Here are the registered sources:

7
00:00:31,905 --> 00:00:38,145
four file sources, and a Postgres database holding refunds and support tickets.

8
00:00:38,145 --> 00:00:40,594
The agent has exactly one tool:

9
00:00:40,594 --> 00:00:42,094
run SQL on Zetaris.

10
00:00:42,794 --> 00:00:45,602
In the WhyDrop app, I ask in plain English:

11
00:00:45,602 --> 00:00:47,822
why did revenue drop in September?

12
00:00:48,522 --> 00:00:50,717
This is a real run, sped up.

13
00:00:50,717 --> 00:00:54,087
First, a fixed signal scan runs on Zetaris.

14
00:00:54,087 --> 00:01:02,710
Then the Nemotron planner proposes competing hypotheses, using memories of past investigations from Meterless.

15
00:01:02,710 --> 00:01:11,020
Each hypothesis is tested with real SQL, including joins between the Postgres refunds and the order files.

16
00:01:11,020 --> 00:01:15,880
The routing panel shows which Nemotron model served each step:

17
00:01:15,880 --> 00:01:21,054
Super plans and analyses, Nano writes SQL, and Lightning verifies.

18
00:01:21,754 --> 00:01:24,053
The answer is ranked by impact.

19
00:01:24,053 --> 00:01:28,502
Every claim cites the exact query, row and column behind it.

20
00:01:28,502 --> 00:01:37,846
Claims that fail verification are struck out, and hypotheses the data contradicts are listed as rejected, with their evidence.

21
00:01:38,546 --> 00:01:44,004
Before any claim is shown, its query is re-run on Zetaris and must reproduce.

22
00:01:44,004 --> 00:01:52,438
Each cited cell must match, every number must be grounded in the data, and an independent Nemotron verifier must agree.

23
00:01:53,138 --> 00:01:59,193
We tested it on five questions over a seeded dataset with three planted causes:

24
00:01:59,193 --> 00:02:06,704
a broken Meta campaign sending clicks to 404 pages, a price rise, and a corrupted product release.

25
00:02:06,704 --> 00:02:11,762
On the latest run it found all three, and passed five out of five.

26
00:02:11,762 --> 00:02:16,591
Results vary between runs, and every run is logged in the repo.

27
00:02:17,291 --> 00:02:26,256
Zetaris for federated data, Meterless for long-term memory, and NVIDIA Nemotron for reasoning, with the routing visible throughout.

28
00:02:26,256 --> 00:02:27,351
Built in Cursor.

29
00:02:28,051 --> 00:02:28,731
WhyDrop.

30
00:02:28,731 --> 00:02:33,317
The code is on GitHub at jamesbrowndevs slash whydrop.
